operating-system-1
==================

A Linux-based operating-system project working toward a cohesive,
macOS-inspired desktop experience with original system applications and assets.
This is not macOS, is not affiliated with Apple, and does not provide macOS
binary compatibility.

Current development stage: Phase 3 / P3.1 original desktop-shell prototype
IMPLEMENTATION IN PROGRESS; NOT QUALIFIED. P3.1 is a separate experimental
Sway DRM/Pixman + original GTK3 GtkLayerShell top bar, Dock, and launcher,
built on the Phase 2 kernel/distribution foundation. It is not yet an accepted
desktop release; all P3-01 through P3-18 gates in docs/os/PHASE_03.md remain
PENDING until evidence establishes otherwise.

Phase 2's reference commit af6f0137132deb8b6f0d2d7e38584974478eff6a
passed two complete independent Phase 2 workflow attempts and its same-SHA
Phase 1 regression. That result does not qualify later source revisions,
including Phase 3. Final P3.1 acceptance requires two clean Phase 3 attempts
and successful Phase 1 and Phase 2 regressions for the exact same SHA.

Phase 3 experimental workflow:
https://github.com/boop-bip80/operating-system-1/actions/workflows/phase3-desktop.yml
Phase 2 qualification workflow:
https://github.com/boop-bip80/operating-system-1/actions/workflows/phase2-desktop.yml
Same-commit Phase 1 regression workflow:
https://github.com/boop-bip80/operating-system-1/actions/workflows/phase1-boot.yml

Phase 1 -- kernel-to-shell foundation VERIFIED
on reference commit 4cc3727310198a1df85f47a818873477bcd31f5b.
Two clean GitHub Actions attempts passed the full kernel/BusyBox build and four
QEMU boot scenarios; the acceptance record and downloadable evidence are in
docs/os/PHASE_01.md. Each newer commit must pass its own CI validation.
Phase 2 adds signed-snapshot Debian userspace, apt/dpkg, a standalone UEFI
disk image with this repository's kernel, and a non-root Weston/Wayland
desktop with a terminal. That reference image deliberately has no custom
shell. Phase 3 develops a separate experimental shell; neither phase qualifies
an installer, live ISO, macOS app compatibility or bare-metal deployment.

Phase 2 quick start (isolated Linux x86-64 build VM)
--------------------------------------------------

Install the prerequisites and read the privilege/safety notes in
docs/os/PHASE_02.md, then run from the repository root:

    make -C distro/phase2 check
    make -C distro/phase2 kernel
    sudo make -C distro/phase2 userspace image
    make -C distro/phase2 manifest test verify
    bash distro/phase2/scripts/boot-qemu.sh --mode run --vnc 1

Connect a VNC viewer to 127.0.0.1:5901. The demo desktop account has no sudo
access; both desktop and root passwords are locked. The default launch does
not expose VNC; the optional listener above is bound only to localhost.
Output lives in .build/phase2/. The image builder creates regular files,
never formats host disks, and uses no loop devices or host mounts.

Phase 2 acceptance boots through OVMF/GRUB without QEMU -kernel or -initrd.
It captures actual screen output, injects unpredictable keyboard/mouse input,
verifies saved data on a second boot, and checks a missing-GPU failure case.

Phase 1 quick start (Linux x86-64)
---------------------------------

Install the host prerequisites listed in docs/os/PHASE_01.md, then run from
the repository root:

    make -C distro/phase1 check
    make -C distro/phase1 build
    make -C distro/phase1 test
    make -C distro/phase1 run

The build uses this repository's kernel and a pinned, statically linked
BusyBox userspace. The automated tests boot in QEMU software emulation (TCG),
so they do not require KVM, a privileged VM runner, or access to host disks.
All build output and disposable virtual storage remain in .build/phase1/.

The test suite covers two boots with ext4 persistence, DHCP networking, an
actual interactive serial shell session, clean poweroff, and a deliberately
broken initramfs. A successful QEMU exit alone is not considered a pass.

